<PublicationLayout
  category="Engineering"
  type="Technical method"
  title="Checking multiple-testing procedures against their original papers"
  description="Reviewing six original papers clarified multiple-testing guarantees and exposed a numerical table entry that disagrees with its defining equation."
  published="September 7, 2026"
  updated="September 8, 2026"
  status="Independently reviewed SR-C source evidence with bounded acceptance recorded; Release 3 preparation"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="current-status">
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <p>
      Six original papers now have a completed primary-source review in nomue Protocol's
      preparation for multi-group comparisons. The review checked the recorded claims
      against the original pages and independently recomputed numerical evidence. It
      returned GO within that six-paper scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      The resulting proposal in <a href={`${REPO}/pull/196`}>PR #196</a> treats one
      source requirement as ready for closure, with a separate review supporting that
      treatment. The subsequent cumulative source record documents limited steward acceptance of SR-C; this is acceptance of the bounded source treatment, not adoption of the methods. This is public
      research evidence for Release 3 preparation; current product support remains
      Release 1 Welch Record verification.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sources">
    <h2>What the originals resolved</h2>
    <p>
      Our <a href="/engineering/cataloguing-multi-group-procedures/">earlier catalogue</a>
      covered 49 multi-group procedures. Many entries still depended on unread sources.
      This follow-up examined papers by Simes, Hochberg, Hommel, Shaffer, Rom, and
      Holland–Copenhaver to determine exactly which claims their procedures support.
    </p>
    <p>
      Multiple testing asks how to control errors when a family contains several
      hypotheses. Procedures with similar names or thresholds can make different
      promises. A result about whether any hypothesis in a family is false does not
      automatically justify naming individual false hypotheses. A guarantee proved
      under independence also cannot be silently extended to dependent tests.
    </p>
    <p>
      The source review made those distinctions explicit. Simes's global test and its
      exploratory individual rejections are separated. Hochberg and Hommel retain the
      validity conditions required by the Simes tests used in their arguments. Shaffer's
      procedures distinguish how many hypotheses can remain true from which specific
      hypotheses have already been rejected.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those details affect executable rules. Hommel's shortcut uses a strict comparison
      during its search and a non-strict comparison for final rejection. Its general
      procedure and its logically restricted variant also give different results in
      the paper's example: three rejections and five, respectively. The review checked
      both against the printed example. Adjusted-output formulas derived by the
      investigators remain labelled as derivations, separate from statements printed
      in the papers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="table">
    <h2>When a table disagrees with its equation</h2>
    <p>
      Rom's 1990 paper defines critical values through a recurrence and presents a table
      obtained from it. In Table 1 on page 664, the modified-Hochberg entry for ten tests
      at α = 0.01 prints <code>1.01 × 10⁻³</code>. Recomputing from equation (2) gives
      <code>0.001004472598983613…</code>, which rounds to <code>1.00 × 10⁻³</code> at
      the table's three significant digits.
    </p>
    <p>
      The primary-source reviewer checked the page image, confirmed the discrepancy,
      and independently integrated the relevant order-statistic probability using exact
      polynomial arithmetic. The recurrence constants satisfied the defining probability
      identity exactly for one through ten tests. The other nineteen modified-Hochberg
      cells agreed at their printed precision. The conflict is therefore recorded as a
      disagreement within the source, rather than attributed to PDF extraction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The approved bounded treatment preserves both values in the evidence record. It
      uses the defining equation and procedure to characterize the method, while
      excluding the printed table as authority for future numerical constants. Any
      implementation would require separately reviewed constants and numerical evidence.
      No formal erratum was verified, and this work makes no claim to be the first to
      have noticed the discrepancy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="protocol">
    <h2>What this advances in nomue</h2>
    <p>
      The practical gain is a more precise basis for deciding what a future verifier
      could promise. Source statements, investigator derivations, and conflicting
      numerical evidence now have distinct recorded roles. A later implementation can
      be reviewed against those roles instead of inheriting an ambiguous method name
      or copying a table without checking its definition.
    </p>
    <p>
      PR #196 applies that treatment to the source requirement labelled SR-C. Its
      original candidate ledger recorded four requirements marked CLOSED and ten still incomplete;
      that historical count is not a count of four formally accepted hold
      closures. The subsequent bounded SR-C acceptance leaves the overall source set
      incomplete and the narrowed program scope remains in place. Selecting a procedure,
      proving its implementation, and opening a Release 3 public RFC are separate steps.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p>Our follow-up, <a href="/engineering/what-multiple-comparison-procedures-guarantee/">What a multiple-comparison procedure actually guarantees</a>, extends the source work to dependence assumptions, ordered comparisons and simultaneous intervals. Its latest repair and disposition status are stated separately.</p>

  <section id="evidence">
    <h2>Evidence and review</h2>
    <p>
      The primary-source completion review in PR #195 checked six supplied originals
      against recorded file hashes and printed-page references. It reported no blocker
      or should-fix finding within that scope. PR #197 separately reviewed PR #196's
      application of the approved treatment and returned GO for that change; it also
      identified a public-language repair in the cited approval record outside the change.
    </p>
    <p>
      Authoring and review used separate LLM-assisted contexts. The records disclose
      author-side OpenAI assistance, reviewer-side model provenance, and the steward's
      scoped independence decision. The steward retains responsibility for each bounded acceptance.
      These are project review records, not journal peer review. The papers themselves
      are not redistributed in the repository.
    </p>
